  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss over x, z from -2 to 2. It is steeper along x, so overshooting starts there when the learning rate is above 1/3. Swings grow when it is above 2/3.",
    "Step = -learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is random with mean 0 and size 2·√((64−b)/(63b)) for batch size b, so it is exactly 0 at 64. Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at 2 × their true step length. The orange arrow is the next actual step. The grey arrow is learning rate × the true downhill direction. The step-length readout gives the true length.",
    "A run stops after 40 steps (finished), or is marked diverged when the loss goes past 8 or the ball leaves the drawn square. The ball and paths are then held at the bowl's edge, while the loss readout keeps its true value.",
    "The bowl shape is exact in this model. With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average, and a single step can raise it.",
    "Changing a setting mid-run takes effect from the next step.",
    "The run advances 5 steps per second at speed 1. Use the card's play, pause, step and speed controls."
  ]
}